      "question": {
        ":question/id": "6fd11f77-744b-5b22-8ddd-792fcb9554a2",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{{e}^{2x}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| ${y}^{\u2032}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $x{e}^{2x}$ |",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$ and the denominator $v(x)={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,{v}^{\u2032}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =\\frac{{u}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{\u2032}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})({e}^{2x})-(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)(2{e}^{2x})}{{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{x}-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)}{{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{e}^{2x}-2x{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{x})}{{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}(1-2x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)}{x{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{x{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
